  "prose": "He went back the way he had come, hand on the wall, and the narrow passage gave him up its bends in reverse until the niche chamber's cold quiet opened around him once more. The skull rows watched him pass with their one shared facing. At the junction he paused, back to the wall a last moment, and the dragging was still there — out in the gray, unchanged, patient.\n\nThen he moved toward the main gallery, careful, staff ready, weight rolling heel to toe on the worn floor.\n\nThe gray half-light grew as he went. It came from somewhere ahead and left — a pale seep around a corner of the gallery, like moon-fall through a broken roof, and he kept to the deep shadow of the near wall and worked toward the corner's edge. The dragging sound strengthened with each step. Drag, settle. Drag, settle. Close now. Close enough that he could hear a second note under it: a faint dry click, bone on stone, riding each settle.\n\nHe put his eye to the corner's edge.\n\nThe gallery opened into a long hall under that pale seep, and the thing was there in the middle distance, walking its slow circuit.\n\nIt was a skeleton, but wrong for the word. The lesser restless dead he had broken already had been man-sized, hurried, empty. This one had been built to a larger plan — head nearly brushing the gallery's high ceiling, limbs long and heavy-boned, its frame pieced and patched with added stock: extra vertebrae worked into the spine, a second layer of rib, one arm ending in a great cleaved scapula it used as a crutch-blade, which was the drag. It went its round of the hall at a walk, foot and blade-edge, foot and blade-edge, past a dais at the far end where a stone stair went down and stopped at a cold sealed face he knew the feel of already. Around its neck, dull in the gray light, hung a ring of old keys on a chain.\n\nIt did not look toward the corner. It walked its round, and the light fell on it, and it passed behind a pillar, and came out again, and walked.\n\nTwenty paces of open floor lay between the corner and the hall's nearest cover.",
